1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,555
This video shows you how to choose a short track speed skating blade that suits you, so you can buy once and buy well.

2
00:00:07,555 --> 00:00:12,293
We will cover flex, length, steel, bend, cups and mounting, one at a time.

3
00:00:12,293 --> 00:00:16,326
Your blades are the only contact point between you and the ice.

4
00:00:16,326 --> 00:00:24,330
They decide how you carry speed, how you hold a line through a corner, and how confident you feel when you commit to an apex.

5
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:28,107
A blade that suits your style feels like part of your body.

6
00:00:28,107 --> 00:00:31,309
A blade that does not will fight you on every lap.

7
00:00:32,009 --> 00:00:34,683
Boots, bearings and suits all play a part.

8
00:00:34,683 --> 00:00:37,802
But the blade is where your power becomes motion.

9
00:00:37,802 --> 00:00:41,367
A good blade turns your push cleanly into forward speed.

10
00:00:41,367 --> 00:00:44,804
It keeps a stable edge when you load it in the corner.

11
00:00:44,804 --> 00:00:48,942
And it releases smoothly as you move from one stride to the next.

12
00:00:48,942 --> 00:00:52,252
Short track is raced on tight corners at high speed.

13
00:00:52,252 --> 00:00:59,637
So small differences in flex, bend and steel quality make large differences in how the skate behaves under pressure.

14
00:01:00,337 --> 00:01:04,804
Flex is how much the blade bends along its length when you load it.

15
00:01:04,804 --> 00:01:06,738
Most skaters notice it first.

16
00:01:06,738 --> 00:01:09,605
A more flexible blade moves with your push.

17
00:01:09,605 --> 00:01:13,539
It absorbs small mistakes in technique and feels forgiving.

18
00:01:13,539 --> 00:01:17,673
It lets the skate flow through a corner with a natural rhythm.

19
00:01:17,673 --> 00:01:20,673
A stiffer blade returns energy more directly.

20
00:01:20,673 --> 00:01:25,941
It feels sharp, holds a very firm line, and responds the moment you drive hard.

21
00:01:25,941 --> 00:01:28,608
Neither one is better in absolute terms.

22
00:01:28,608 --> 00:01:34,275
The right flex depends on your weight, your power, and how you like a corner to feel.

23
00:01:34,275 --> 00:01:38,943
As a general rule, lighter and more technical skaters enjoy more flex.

24
00:01:38,943 --> 00:01:43,277
Heavier and more powerful skaters tend to want a firmer platform.

25
00:01:43,977 --> 00:01:46,818
Here is the range on a single flex scale.

26
00:01:46,818 --> 00:01:51,252
On the left, in green, is the soft end: more flex and more feel.

27
00:01:51,252 --> 00:01:56,726
On the right, in orange, is the firm end: more hold and a more direct response.

28
00:01:56,726 --> 00:01:58,943
The JaeBee sits at the soft end.

29
00:01:58,943 --> 00:02:05,109
Its tube tapers toward the ends, so the tips give more and the flex builds progressively.

30
00:02:05,109 --> 00:02:13,840
In the middle is a blade with even stiffness along its full length, a stable platform for skaters who want a little more hold.

31
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:20,352
At the firm end is the XC Flex Weapon, built for maximum corner hold while still gliding well.

32
00:02:20,352 --> 00:02:23,817
Pick your spot on this line by feel, flow or hold.

33
00:02:24,517 --> 00:02:25,475
Next is length.

34
00:02:25,475 --> 00:02:30,137
You choose it to match your boot size, your height and your racing style.

35
00:02:30,137 --> 00:02:33,714
Shorter blades change direction quickly and feel lively.

36
00:02:33,714 --> 00:02:35,949
That suits tight, reactive skating.

37
00:02:35,949 --> 00:02:40,356
Longer blades track straighter and carry more speed on the straights.

38
00:02:40,356 --> 00:02:43,869
That suits smooth, powerful skaters who like stability.

39
00:02:43,869 --> 00:02:49,937
Most skaters settle within a narrow window that fits their boot, and then fine tune from there.

40
00:02:49,937 --> 00:02:51,534
Do not guess your length.

41
00:02:51,534 --> 00:02:56,451
If you are unsure, send your boot size to the shop and ask which length fits.

42
00:02:57,152 --> 00:03:00,132
The runner is the part that touches the ice.

43
00:03:00,132 --> 00:03:04,196
Its steel decides how long your edge lasts and how it feels.

44
00:03:04,196 --> 00:03:07,719
Bimetal runners are around 62 on the hardness scale.

45
00:03:07,719 --> 00:03:15,441
They give an excellent balance of performance, durability and value, and they are a smart choice for most skaters.

46
00:03:15,441 --> 00:03:18,421
Full PM steel is around 64, so it is harder.

47
00:03:18,421 --> 00:03:21,740
It holds a sharp edge longer between sharpenings.

48
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:28,920
If you train a lot and hate losing your edge in the middle of a session, the harder steel pays for itself.

49
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:32,917
You sharpen less often, and the feel stays more consistent.

50
00:03:33,617 --> 00:03:38,691
Short track blades are bent to a radius so the skate can carve a tight corner.

51
00:03:38,691 --> 00:03:42,205
The accuracy and smoothness of that bend matter a lot.

52
00:03:42,205 --> 00:03:47,539
A precise bend gives a predictable arc that repeats every time you load the blade.

53
00:03:47,539 --> 00:03:51,183
That builds confidence when you lean at full commitment.

54
00:03:51,183 --> 00:03:53,525
Cups connect the blade to your boot.

55
00:03:53,525 --> 00:03:57,363
Light, strong aluminium cups add stiffness and save weight.

56
00:03:57,363 --> 00:04:03,674
They come in different heights, so you can tune how far you lean before the boot touches the ice.

57
00:04:03,674 --> 00:04:06,081
A clean, straight mount is essential.

58
00:04:06,081 --> 00:04:10,310
If the blade is even slightly off, your edges feel unpredictable.

59
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:14,734
Take your time with alignment, or ask an experienced skater to help.

60
00:04:15,434 --> 00:04:18,610
This side view shows how the parts fit together.

61
00:04:18,610 --> 00:04:20,463
At the top is the boot sole.

62
00:04:20,463 --> 00:04:22,249
Two cups drop down from it.

63
00:04:22,249 --> 00:04:26,815
They are the aluminium blocks that hold the blade and set its height.

64
00:04:26,815 --> 00:04:33,167
Below the cups is the tube, shown in blue, with the lips in green where the tube meets the cups.

65
00:04:33,167 --> 00:04:37,070
At the bottom is the steel runner, bent to a corner radius.

66
00:04:37,070 --> 00:04:40,643
Every part in this chain affects how the edge behaves.

67
00:04:40,643 --> 00:04:45,738
So when something feels wrong on the ice, check each part from the boot down.

68
00:04:46,438 --> 00:04:51,473
This is the question skaters forget to ask until the parcel is open.

69
00:04:51,473 --> 00:04:53,842
A blade is not a universal part.

70
00:04:53,842 --> 00:04:59,469
Short track boots use 165 millimetre spacing between the two mounting holes.

71
00:04:59,469 --> 00:05:02,949
Inline speed boots are commonly drilled at 195.

72
00:05:02,949 --> 00:05:06,651
If those do not match, the parts will not line up.

73
00:05:06,651 --> 00:05:12,723
An adapter set bridges 195 down to 165 using the existing holes, with no drilling.

74
00:05:12,723 --> 00:05:17,609
So measure between the centres of your two holes before you order.

75
00:05:17,609 --> 00:05:19,757
The lower line is cup height.

76
00:05:19,757 --> 00:05:23,829
Lower sits you closer to the ice and feels more stable.

77
00:05:23,829 --> 00:05:27,605
Higher gives a deeper lean before the boot touches.

78
00:05:27,605 --> 00:05:34,861
If you change height, go one millimetre at a time, and give it a few sessions before you judge it.

79
00:05:35,561 --> 00:05:42,117
If you are newer to short track, choose a forgiving, flexible blade and a length that fits your boot.

80
00:05:42,117 --> 00:05:45,428
A friendly platform helps you build good technique.

81
00:05:45,428 --> 00:05:49,908
Intermediate skaters can start choosing flex by the feel they prefer.

82
00:05:49,908 --> 00:05:52,699
Lean flexible for flow, or firmer for hold.

83
00:05:52,699 --> 00:05:59,385
Elite racers usually know what they want, and pick steel and cup height to match their setup and event.

84
00:05:59,385 --> 00:06:01,528
Whatever you choose, care for it.

85
00:06:01,528 --> 00:06:06,137
Sharpen consistently with a proper jig that clamps the blades straight.

86
00:06:06,137 --> 00:06:09,383
Wear cut resistant gloves when you work on blades.

87
00:06:09,383 --> 00:06:13,213
Dry your blades after every session, and check your mounts.

88
00:06:13,913 --> 00:06:16,420
A few mistakes come up again and again.

89
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:21,499
Skaters chase the blade a champion uses, rather than the blade that suits them.

90
00:06:21,499 --> 00:06:25,935
They pick a length by guesswork instead of matching it to their boot.

91
00:06:25,935 --> 00:06:30,049
They ignore steel quality, and then wonder why their edge fades.

92
00:06:30,049 --> 00:06:34,164
And they skip maintenance, which quietly wears down performance.

93
00:06:34,164 --> 00:06:39,114
Avoid these four, and you will get far more out of whatever blade you choose.

94
00:06:39,114 --> 00:06:39,949
One more tip.

95
00:06:39,949 --> 00:06:43,421
Blades and cups can move to a new pair of boots later.

96
00:06:43,421 --> 00:06:46,957
Just check the mounting spacing on the new boots first.

97
00:06:47,657 --> 00:06:49,647
So here is the short version.

98
00:06:49,647 --> 00:06:51,981
Choose flex by feel, flow or hold.

99
00:06:51,981 --> 00:06:54,040
Match the length to your boot.

100
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:56,305
Pick steel by how much you train.

101
00:06:56,305 --> 00:07:02,276
Check your mounting spacing before you order, and change cup height one step at a time.

102
00:07:02,276 --> 00:07:09,552
The full guide is on the LiveLica blog, with more answers on length, cups and moving blades between boots.

103
00:07:09,552 --> 00:07:11,679
The link is in the description.
